Activity 1: Internet Protocols tutorial. I am describing the TCP/IP protocol suite. I learned that the Internet works because of protocols that each handle one job. IP gives every device an address and moves packets between networks. TCP sits on top of IP and makes sure the data arrives complete and in order, while UDP is faster but does not check for delivery. I also learned that protocols such as HTTP, FTP, and SMTP run on top of these for web pages, file transfers, and email. I can apply this when I build web pages for this class, because I now understand what happens between my browser and GitHub when a page loads, and it will help me make sense of errors when a page does not load.

Activity 2: Review IP address settings using ipconfig. The topic I am describing is IP addressing and DHCP. I learned that my computer needs four main settings to get online: an IP address, a subnet mask, a default gateway, and a DNS server. My router hands these out automatically through DHCP, and the address is leased for a limited time instead of being assigned permanently. Releasing and renewing the lease asks the router for a fresh address. I also learned that my home address is a private address, which is different from the public address my Internet provider gives my router. I can apply this at home whenever a device will not connect, because I can check whether it actually received an address before blaming my Internet provider.

Activity 3: Verify Internet connectivity using ping, tracert, and nslookup. The topic I am describing is network troubleshooting. I learned that connection problems can be narrowed down by testing in order from close to far. Pinging my own address tests my computer, pinging the default gateway tests my local network, and pinging 8.8.8.8 tests the Internet connection. Tracert shows each router a packet passes through on the way to its destination, which shows where a delay or failure happens. Nslookup checks whether DNS can turn a name like en.wikiversity.org into an IP address. If I can ping 8.8.8.8 but a website name will not load, the problem is DNS and not the connection. I can apply this at home and later in radiology, where imaging equipment sends images over the hospital network, so I can tell IT whether a problem is with one machine or with the connection.
